WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.762
On this slide, we are focusing on pivot tables.

00:00:02.762 --> 00:00:07.512
Start with the meaning of the idea before worrying about the exact syntax.

00:00:07.512 --> 00:00:13.772
Ask what information is going in, what Python is doing with it, and what result should come out.

00:00:13.772 --> 00:00:18.157
A pivot table summarizes a numeric measure across categories.

00:00:18.157 --> 00:00:24.947
Always be clear about what the rows, columns, and cell values represent.

00:00:24.947 --> 00:00:27.217
Before clicking Run, predict how the table should change.

00:00:27.217 --> 00:00:32.752
Then run the cell and inspect the row count, column values, grouping, dates, missing values,

00:00:32.752 --> 00:00:38.192
or flags that matter. Change one input and rerun it.

00:00:38.192 --> 00:00:44.372
Connect the DataFrame operation to an analytical question rather than simply making the code execute.
